{
  "id": "deepest-leaves-sum",
  "questionId": "deepest-leaves-sum",
  "slug": "deepest-leaves-sum",
  "title": "Deepest Leaves Sum",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Tree bfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree bfs",
    "queue"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "since the deepest level is always the last one your BFS finishes processing, do you need to detect 'deepest' explicitly at all?",
    "run a level order BFS, computing each level's total sum as you go, the same way you would for summing every level",
    "instead of collecting all the level sums, just keep overwriting a single 'last level's sum' variable",
    "when the queue empties, that last recorded sum is exactly the deepest leaves' sum"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "accumulating a running total across ALL levels instead of resetting the sum for each new level",
    "only summing actual 'leaf' nodes (nodes with no children) instead of every node on the deepest level, which happens to be the same thing here but is easy to overcomplicate",
    "trying to precompute the max depth in a separate pass first, adding unnecessary complexity compared to just tracking the last level's sum during one BFS pass"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A binary tree is given as a plain integer array named `tree`, using a\ncomplete-binary-tree (heap) index layout:\n\n- the root sits at index 0\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- a child index only counts as real if it's within the array bounds **and** its value isn't the sentinel `-101`\n- `-101` means \"no node here\" - real node values always fall between -100 and 100, so -101 can never be a genuine value, and an index at or past the end of the array also counts as missing\n\nFor example, `[1, 2, 3, -101, 4]` encodes this tree:\n\n```\n      1\n     / \\\n    2   3\n     \\\n      4\n```\n\nNode 2 has no left child (sentinel) but does have a right child, 4.\n\n**Find the tree's deepest level, the level furthest from the root that still has at least one real node, and return the sum of all node values at that level.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= length of tree <= 2000",
      "every real node value is in the range -100 to 100",
      "the sentinel value -101 marks a missing node and never appears as a real value",
      "the tree always has at least one real node"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single-node tree, whose sum is just that node's value",
      "a deepest level with only one real node",
      "negative values at the deepest level"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [3, 9, 20, -101, -101, 15, 7]",
        "output": "22",
        "expectedOutput": "22",
        "explanation": "the deepest level is level 2, containing 15 and 7, which sum to 22"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]",
        "output": "22",
        "expectedOutput": "22",
        "explanation": "the deepest level is level 2, containing 4, 5, 6, 7, which sum to 22"
      },
